  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height over x, z from -2 to 2. It is steeper along x (left-right), so steps there overshoot first: with no noise, rates above 2/3 make the x swings grow.",
    "Each step = -learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch b: largest at 1, exactly 0 at 64.",
    "Purple arrow: the next mini-batch step. Dark arrow: the step the full data would give. Both point the way the ball moves and are drawn at 2× their true length.",
    "Diverged: the loss goes above 8 (the highest loss on the drawn bowl) or the ball leaves the bowl; that ends the run, as does reaching 40 steps. Off-bowl points are drawn at the rim.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed and an index that keeps counting across new runs. Reset sets everything back to its default and replays the same noise from the start.",
    "Each step lowers the loss only on average. With small batches a single step can raise it."
  ]
}